VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-025
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of profound physical collapse, severe cognitive impairment, and total loss of ADL function following minor exertion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute, severe physical and neurological crash rendering the patient unable to speak in full sentences, chew food, or tolerate light. Immediate physical assessment was mandated to rule out acute encephalopathy, stroke, or severe metabolic derangement before regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I pushed myself yesterday... now my body is lead, my brain is offline, and I can barely lift my head.
History of Present Illness: Patient with a post-viral ME/CFS phenotype reports a devastating crash triggered by a minor physical outing yesterday. The patient is experiencing severe Post-Exertional Malaise (PEM). Symptoms include crushing, paralytic fatigue, profoundly unrefreshing sleep (slept 14 hours with zero recovery), and severe cognitive impairment (brain fog, aphasia, slowed processing). The patient describes feeling as though their cells have completely lost the ability to generate energy.
Functional Status: Strictly bedbound. Unable to independently sit up, eat solid food, or engage in sustained conversation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Highly dependent on partner for basic hydration, feeding, and turning.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 mmHg | HR: 65 bpm | Temp: 36.4 C | RR: 14 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Profoundly lethargic, wearing sunglasses in a dimly lit room, whispering to converse to conserve energy.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals grossly intact cranial nerves, symmetrical reflexes, and normal (though weak) motor tone; no focal deficits indicating stroke. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are unremarkable. Cervical lymph nodes are mildly palpable and tender. Presentation is the archetypal post-viral energy limitation crash (PEM).
Environmental Observations: Room is blacked out and silent. Partner is feeding the patient liquids via a straw.
Medication Reconciliation: No new medications. Intolerant to standard stimulants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Long COVID ME/CFS Phenotype - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Global cognitive slowing and symmetric weakness, rather than focal, unilateral neurological deficits.

Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal core temperature, absence of tachycardia or tachypnea, and normal peripheral perfusion.

Severe Major Depressive Episode - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear timeline linking the acute physiological collapse directly to an identifiable physical exertion trigger (PEM), rather than a primary psychological origin.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute state of near-paralytic fatigue and cognitive failure required an immediate face-to-face evaluation to explicitly exclude life-threatening neurological events (TIA/stroke) and acute metabolic crises. Having confirmed hemodynamic stability and the absence of focal neurological deficits, it was clinically determined the patient was in a state of severe cellular energy failure (PEM). Aggressive radical rest in the home was prescribed; transferring this patient to an emergency department would have massively exacerbated the sensory and metabolic crash.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed absolute sensory and physical rest. Targeted intervention strategy includes LDN, NAD+, CoQ10, and Wahls Diet protocols to be considered once baseline is re-established. Advised partner on maintaining oral hydration via electrolyte solutions.
Positive Behavioral Change: By enforcing radical rest with an eye mask and earplugs for the next 24 hours without attempting to process information, you will actively reduce your brain's energy consumption, directly allowing your mitochondria to recharge and rewarding yourself with a faster exit from the crash.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely; patient requires absolute minimal stimulation.
DME/Safety Optional: Bedpan or bedside commode to prevent energy expenditure for toileting.
Follow-up: Routine GP review via Telehealth in 1 week (in-person visits contraindicated due to PEM risk).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if the patient becomes completely unrousable, develops a sudden high fever, or stops breathing normally.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Post-Exertional Malaise reflects a profound metabolic dysfunction; there is no acute pharmaceutical cure for a crash, only time and rest.
Clinical Course: This severe crash may last days to weeks. The patient's baseline function will return extremely slowly provided zero further energy expenditure is demanded of them.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Partner must call 000 if the patient cannot be woken up at all, or if one side of their face droops or their breathing becomes extremely shallow.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your cellular batteries are completely depleted, and pushing through is metabolically impossible. The only treatment right now is turning off all sensory input to let the batteries slowly recharge.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to read, look at screens, or hold conversations tomorrow; honoring this absolute cognitive rest prevents further cellular energy drain, positively reinforcing a safer and shorter recovery period.

